1
00:00:06,319 --> 00:00:08,250
A knife cannot cut itself.

2
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:10,388
So how could a mind know itself?

3
00:00:11,209 --> 00:00:15,640
This is where two traditions meet,
and where they rub against each other.

4
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:19,947
Madhyamaka, the Middle
Way, refuses svabhāva,

5
00:00:20,027 --> 00:00:22,080
a nature that stands on its own.

6
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:26,428
Dzogchen speaks of rang rig,
self-cognizing awareness.

7
00:00:26,508 --> 00:00:30,816
And a nature that is not produced,
yet is the ground of appearances,

8
00:00:30,896 --> 00:00:34,611
can sound very close to
what Madhyamaka refuses.

9
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:40,027
So agreement between the two cannot
be assumed. It has to be argued.

10
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:44,054
Candrakīrti makes the difficulty concrete.

11
00:00:44,134 --> 00:00:46,065
A knife cannot cut itself.

12
00:00:46,145 --> 00:00:50,916
In the same way, a mind cannot know
itself without splitting in two:

13
00:00:50,996 --> 00:00:52,850
a knower, and a known.

14
00:00:53,653 --> 00:00:55,665
Mipham answers with a lamp.

15
00:00:55,745 --> 00:00:58,763
A lamp lights itself while
it lights other things.

16
00:00:58,843 --> 00:01:01,738
No second lamp is needed to see the first.

17
00:01:01,818 --> 00:01:06,440
Self-knowing, on this view, is not a
second act performed on the flow of

18
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:10,448
awareness. It is simply what
the flow's luminosity is,

19
00:01:10,528 --> 00:01:12,137
its knowing clarity.

20
00:01:12,953 --> 00:01:14,707
Mipham went further.

21
00:01:14,787 --> 00:01:19,843
He argued that the luminosity of
rigpa, awareness in its natural state,

22
00:01:19,923 --> 00:01:21,636
is not a svabhāva.

23
00:01:21,716 --> 00:01:24,911
It is not established as
an independent object.

24
00:01:24,991 --> 00:01:30,051
It is known only in self-knowing, and
depends on nothing outside itself.

25
00:01:30,861 --> 00:01:36,460
Gorampa presses the Middle Way's critique
of intrinsic nature more absolutely.

26
00:01:36,540 --> 00:01:40,903
His reading shows where a Dzogchen
formulation risks reification,

27
00:01:40,983 --> 00:01:43,485
turning what is open into a thing.

28
00:01:43,565 --> 00:01:46,419
The debate is useful
for exactly this reason.

29
00:01:46,499 --> 00:01:48,978
Both sides agree that the risk is real.

30
00:01:49,777 --> 00:01:53,406
Longchenpa is explicit.
The ground is empty.

31
00:01:53,486 --> 00:01:57,428
It is not a subtle existent,
standing behind appearances.

32
00:01:57,508 --> 00:02:01,626
Later commentators have argued at length
that the two traditions point to the same

33
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:06,789
thing. On one side, appearance
and emptiness, inseparable.

34
00:02:06,869 --> 00:02:11,236
On the other, primordial purity
and spontaneous presence.

35
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:14,432
That equivalence is still contested.

36
00:02:14,512 --> 00:02:20,125
Some lineages hold that Dzogchen is its
own resultant vehicle, its own path.

37
00:02:20,205 --> 00:02:25,057
It is not reducible to Madhyamaka
analysis, or guaranteed by it.

38
00:02:25,137 --> 00:02:29,622
To say the two refer to one nature
is this book's own synthesis,

39
00:02:29,702 --> 00:02:31,416
and it is offered as one.

40
00:02:31,496 --> 00:02:33,617
The safer claim is narrower.

41
00:02:33,697 --> 00:02:37,367
Both agree that the grasp at
solid substance is the problem.

42
00:02:37,447 --> 00:02:39,980
And their methods converge on undoing it.

43
00:02:40,798 --> 00:02:43,096
They also share a danger in practice.

44
00:02:43,176 --> 00:02:47,145
Dzogchen teachers distinguish
zhig pa, suppression,

45
00:02:47,225 --> 00:02:49,740
from rang grol, self-release.

46
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:51,928
Suppression pushes thoughts down.

47
00:02:52,008 --> 00:02:55,542
It can make a stillness that
becomes one more fixed state.

48
00:02:55,622 --> 00:03:00,369
Self-release lets thoughts dissolve,
when they are not followed or fixed.

49
00:03:01,189 --> 00:03:04,614
Madhyamaka makes the same
point from its own side.

50
00:03:04,694 --> 00:03:09,451
Candrakīrti accepted śamatha,
the practice of calming the mind,

51
00:03:09,531 --> 00:03:11,380
as a conventional means.

52
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:14,410
But for him the decisive
step was analysis.

53
00:03:14,490 --> 00:03:18,219
Analysis shows that the parts
searched for cannot be found.

54
00:03:19,038 --> 00:03:23,039
Stillness forced by effort can
become one more thing to hold.

